Feature: Price alerts
  Approval: approved

  An alert tells you when the rate reaches a target you chose, so you do not have to keep
  looking. You set one target for the pair you are watching. The alert waits until the rate
  reaches it, then shows a banner.

  An alert only reacts to a rate it can trust. While the feed is unavailable there is no rate,
  so the alert keeps waiting and does not fire on an old one. The same goes for a rate that
  arrives while the feed is live: it is checked against your target as soon as it comes.

  Expectation: Setting an alert makes it wait
    Given the alert is off
    When the user sets an alert
    Then the alert is waiting

  Expectation: Reaching the target fires the alert
    The banner appears the moment a live rate reaches your target.
    Given the alert is waiting
    And the rate feed is live
    When the rate reaches the target
    Then the alert is triggered

  Expectation: Without a rate the alert keeps waiting
    No rate means nothing to compare with your target, so the alert stays as it was.
    Given the alert is waiting
    And the rate feed is unavailable
    Then the alert is waiting

  Expectation: A delayed rate can fire the alert
    Approval: proposed
    Unresolved: should an old rate fire an alert? Firing tells you sooner, but the real
    rate may already have moved back. Product has not decided. Until it does, this is
    proposed and an approved manual leaves it out.
    Given the alert is waiting
    And the rate feed is delayed
    When the rate reaches the target
    Then the alert is triggered
